    46	\section{Introduction}
    47	A $ Stiener\  system \ S(2,4,v)$ is a pair $(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{B})$ where $\mathcal{V}$  is a $v$-element set and $\mathcal{ B}$
    48	 is a family of $4$-element subsets of $\mathcal{V}$ called $blocks$, such that each $2$-element subsets of $\mathcal{V}$ is contained in
    49	 exactly one block.
    50	
    51	Two Steiner systems $S(2,4,v),\ (\mathcal{V},\mathcal{B})$ and $\  (\mathcal{V},\mathcal{B}_{1})$ are said to $intersect$ in $s$ blocks if
    52	$|\mathcal{B}\cap\mathcal{B}_{1}|=s$. The intersection problem for $S(2,4,v)$ designs can be extended in this way: determine the sets
    53	$\overline{J_{\mu}[v]}(J_{\mu}[v])$ of all integers $s$ such that there exists a collection of $\mu \ (\geq 2)\ S(2,4,v)$ designs mutually
    54	intersecting in $s$ blocks (in the same set of $s$ blocks). This generalization is called $\mu$-way intersection problem.
    55	Clearly $\overline{J_{2}[v]}=J_2[v]=J[v]$ and $J_{\mu}[v]\subseteq\overline{J_{\mu}[v]}\subseteq J[v]$.
    56	
    57	The intersection problem for $\mu=2$ was considered by Colbourn, Hoffman, and  Lindner in~\cite{2}. They determined
    58	the set $J[v](J_{2}[v])$
    59	completely for all values $v\equiv1, 4\ (\rm mod \ 12)$, with some possible exceptions for $v=$ 25, 28 and 37. Let $[a,b]=\{a,a+1,...,b-1,b\}$, $b_{v}=\frac {v(v-1)}{12}$,
    60	and $I[v]=[0,b_{v}]\setminus( [b_{v}-5,b_{v}-1]\cup \{b_{v}-7\})$. It is shown in~\cite{2}; that:
    61	
    62	$(1)\  J[v]\subseteq I[v]$ for all  $v\equiv1, 4\ (\rm mod\  12)$.
    63	
    64	$(2)\ J[v]=I[v]$ for all admissible $v\geq40$.
    65	
    66	$(3)\ J[13]=I[13]$ and $J[16]=I[16]\setminus\{7,9,10,11,14\}$.
    67	
    68	$(4)\ I[25] \setminus \{31,33,34,37,39,40,41,42,44\} \subseteq J[25]$ and $\{42,44\} \not \subseteq J[25]$.
    69	
    70	$(5)\ I[28]\setminus\{44,46,49,50,52,53,54,57\}\subseteq J[28]$.
    71	
    72	$(6)\ I[37]\setminus(\{64,66,76,82,84,85,88\} \cup [90,94] \cup [96,101])\subseteq J[37]$.
    73	
    74	
    75	Also Chang, Feng, and Lo Faro investigate another type of
    76	intersection which is called triangle intersection (See~\cite{14}).
    77	Milici and Quattrocchi~\cite{4} determined $J_{3}[v]$ for $STS$s.
    78	Other results about the intersection problem can be found in
    79	$\cite{22,23,24,25,26,27,28}$. In this paper we investigate the
    80	three way intersection problem for $S(2,4,v)$ designs. We set
    81	$I_{3}[v]=[0,b_{v}]\setminus[b_{v}-7, b_{v}-1]$.  As our main
    82	result, we prove the following theorem.
    83	%
    84	\begin{thm}~\label{1.1}
    85	$(1)\  J_{3}[v]\subseteq I_{3}[v]$ for all  $v\equiv1, 4\ (\rm mod\  12)$.
    86	
    87	$(2)\ J_{3}[v]=I_{3}[v]$ for all admissible $v\geq49$.
    88	
    89	$(3)\  I_{3}[40]\setminus�\{ b_{40}-15, b_{40}-14\}\subseteq J_{3}[40]$.
    90	
    91	$(4)\ J_{3}[13]=I_{3}[13]$ and $J_{3}[16]=I_{3}[16]\setminus\{7,9,10,11,12\}$..
    92	
    93	$(5)\ [0,11] \cup \{13,15,17,20,29,50\} \cup [22,24] \subseteq J_{3}[25]$ and $\{42\}\not \in J_{3}[25]$.
    94	
    95	$(6)\ [1,24]\cup \{27,28,33,37,39,63\}\subseteq J_3[28]$.
    96	
    97	$(7)\ \{18,19,78,79,81,87,102,103,111\} \cup [21,32] \cup [34,36] \cup [38,43] \cup [45,48] \cup [52,54] \cup [58,63] \cup [67,71] \subseteq J_{3}[37]$.
    98	\end{thm}
