   101	\section{Necessary conditions}
   102	In this section we establish necessary conditions for $J_3[v]$. For this purpose, we use another concept that is relative to intersection problem:
   103	A $(v,k,t)$ $trade$ of volume $s$ consists of two disjoint collections  $ T_{1}$ and ${T}_2$, each of $s$ blocks,
   104	such that for every $t$-subset of blocks, the  number of blocks containing these elements ($t$-subset) are the same in both  $T_{1}$ and ${T}_2$.
   105	A  $(v,k,t)$ trade of volume $s$ is $Steiner$ when for  every $t$-subset of blocks, the
   106	number of blocks containing these elements are at most one.
   107	A $\mu$-way $(v,k,t)$ trade ${T}=\{{T}_{1},{T}_{2},\ldots,{T}_{\mu}\}$, $\mu\geq2$ is a set of pairwise disjoint $(v,k,t)$ trade.\\
   108	In every collection the union of blocks must cover the same set of elements. This set of elements is called the \textit{foundation} of the trade. Its notation is found (T) and
   109	$r_{x}=$ no. of blocks in a collection which contain the element $x$.\\
   110	By definition of the trade, if $b_v-s$ is in $J_{3}[v]$, then it is clear that there exists a 3-way  Steiner $(v,4,2)$ trade of volume $s$. Consider three
   111	$S(2,4,v)$ designs (systems) intersecting in $b_v-s$ same blocks (of size four). The remaining set of blocks (of size four) form disjoint partial quadruple systems,
   112	containing precisely the same pairs, and each has $s$ blocks. Rashidi and  Soltankhah in~\cite{15} established that there do not exist a 3-way Steiner $(v,4,2)$ trade
   113	of volume $s$, for  $s\in \{1,2,3,4,5,6,7\}$.
   114	So we have the following lemma:
   115	\begin{lem}~\label{2.1}
   116	$J_{3}[v]\subseteq I_3[v]$.
   117	\end{lem}
